I originally got this when trying to apply the following simplification to cg_clif's mini_core:

```diff
diff --git a/example/mini_core.rs b/example/mini_core.rs
index 59fdca70..25e7484b 100644
--- a/example/mini_core.rs
+++ b/example/mini_core.rs
@@ -640,10 +640,7 @@ pub fn new(val: T) -> Box<T> {
             intrinsics::copy(&val as *const T as *const u8, ptr, size);
             Box(
                 Unique {
-                    pointer: NonNull(intrinsics::transmute::<
-                        *mut u8,
-                        pattern_type!(*const T is !null),
-                    >(ptr)),
+                    pointer: intrinsics::transmute::<*mut u8, NonNull<T>>(ptr),
                     _marker: PhantomData,
                 },
                 Global,
@@ -656,9 +653,7 @@ impl<T: ?Sized, A> Drop for Box<T, A> {
     fn drop(&mut self) {
         // inner value is dropped by compiler
         unsafe {
-            libc::free(intrinsics::transmute::<pattern_type!(*const T is !null), *const T>(
-                self.0.pointer.0,
-            ) as *mut u8);
+            libc::free(intrinsics::transmute::<NonNull<T>, *mut u8>(self.0.pointer));
         }
     }
 }
```

## Coming at this from the `Try` perspective

I would expect `my_poll?` to be the equivalent of `ready!(mypoll)` - thus allowing the `ready!` macro to go the same way that the `try!` macro has.

That would mean we had (possible typos as I'm doing this outside an IDE and deliberately spelling out the assoc types):
```rust
impl Try for Poll<T> {
    Output = T,
    Residual = Poll<!>

    fn branch (self) -> ControlFlow<Poll<!>, T> {
        self::Ready(t) => ControlFlow::Continue(t),
        self::Pending => ControlFlow::Break(Poll::Pending),
    }

    fn from_output(output: T) -> Self {
        Self::Ready(output)
    }
}

impl FromResidual<Poll<!>> for Poll<T> {
    fn from_residual(residual: Poll<!>) -> Self {
        match residual {
            Poll:Pending => Self::Pending,
        }
    }
}
```

Which would, of course conflict with the two existing specific impls. Thankfully they are both still nightly-only otherwise that would be impossibly breaking for users.

## Looking at where I've used the current impl

it does, sort of make sense ...

```rust
/// The future returned by [UdpStream::next]
#[derive(Debug)]
pub struct Next<'stream, const BUF_SIZE: usize> {
    stream: &'stream mut UdpStream<BUF_SIZE>,
}

impl<'stream, const BUF_SIZE: usize> Future for Next<'stream, BUF_SIZE> {
    type Output = Option<io::Result<([u8; BUF_SIZE], usize, SocketAddr)>>;

    fn poll(mut self: Pin<&mut Self>, cx: &mut Context<'_>) -> Poll<Self::Output> {
        let this = &mut *self;
        let stream = &mut *this.stream;
        ready!(stream.poll_read_ready_unpin(cx)?); // <- ? on a Poll<Result<Self::Ok, Self::Err>> inside a ready!() expansion
```

calling `ready!(poll_wrapping_result_T?)` works inside a function returning `Poll<Result<T>>`

## How could we get same usability and be consistent with Try and the rest of the std lib?

`Option` & `Result` both offer a `transpose` method as well as `Result::ok()`/`Result::err()`/`Option::ok_or()` for handling similar situations.

If we were ok (!) with Poll's assumption that people will always want to pull out the `Result` in a `Poll<Option<Result<T>>>` then it would be easy enough to add a similar  `Poll<Result<T,E>>::transpose(self) -> Result<Poll<T>,E>` along with a `Result<Poll<T>,E>::ok_or_pending(self) -> Poll<T>` (etc...)

That would allow the example above to be explicit:
```rust
/// The future returned by [UdpStream::next]
#[derive(Debug)]
pub struct Next<'stream, const BUF_SIZE: usize> {
    stream: &'stream mut UdpStream<BUF_SIZE>,
}

impl<'stream, const BUF_SIZE: usize> Future for Next<'stream, BUF_SIZE> {
    type Output = Option<io::Result<([u8; BUF_SIZE], usize, SocketAddr)>>;

    fn poll(mut self: Pin<&mut Self>, cx: &mut Context<'_>) -> Poll<Self::Output> {
        let this = &mut *self;
        let stream = &mut *this.stream;
        stream.poll_read_ready_unpin(cx).transpose().ok_or_pending()?;
```

As a user I find this much better as I can also handle cases where I want to map a `Poll<Option<Result::Err>>` to `Poll::Ready<None>` as well as cases where I want to map to `Poll::Pending`

for full homogeneity would it be valuable to also extend `trait Residual` to require:
```rust
pub const trait Residual<O>: Sized {
    /// The "return" type of this meta-function.
    type TryType: [const] Try<Output = O, Residual = Self> + FromResidual<Self>;
                                                             ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^
```

sadly, this would (always) reduce the need to add a bound in generic code but it certainly would help with ensuring consistent implementations

