> the #[derive(MyTrait)] needs to be able to see the #[repr(...)] on the struct definition (I'm assuming that's what currently happens but haven't verified),

```rust
#[proc_macro_derive(PrintIt)]
pub fn assert_return_type_copy(item: TokenStream) -> TokenStream {
    println!("proc_macro_derive: `{item}`");
    TokenStream::new()
}

#[proc_macro_attribute]
pub fn print_it(_attr: proc_macro::TokenStream, item: proc_macro::TokenStream) -> TokenStream {
    println!("proc_macro_attr: `{item}`");
    item
}
```

Then

```rust
#[repr(C)]
#[print_it]
#[derive(PrintIt)]
pub struct Foo;
```
prints 
```
proc_macro_attr: `#[repr(C)] #[derive(PrintIt)] pub struct Foo;` 
proc_macro_derive: `#[repr(C)] pub struct Foo;`
```

So the repr attribute indeed remains visible. When we try to "expand" `NonMacroAttr`s we just insert it back and mark it as inert so that it is not expanded again. But replacing it by a macro would not have that behavior.

We'll also have to handle things like this correctly:
```rust
macro_rules! make_struct {
    ($name:ident) => { pub struct $name; }
}

#[repr(C)]
make_struct!(Bar);
```

Since a `repr` builtin macro would change a field on ast items, this field would also need to survive any expansion steps following it. Otherwise we will run into problems like https://github.com/rust-lang/rust/issues/153745


 If we want to have it "masquerade as an inert attribute", then likely we will need to something similar to regular inert attributes; mark it as inert but actually set it aside to expand it later when everything else has expanded.

I see, makes sense. I guess the confusing part to me is that although you can implement the trait for `&'c u8`, you can't write the function signature the same way it appears in the trait definition. Minimized further:

```rust
trait Trait {
    type Y<'a> where Self: 'a;
    fn get_y<'b>() -> Self::Y<'b>;
}

impl<'c> Trait for &'c u8 {
    type Y<'a> = &'a u8 where Self: 'a;
    // this matches the signature in the trait definition, but won't compile:
    //   fn get_y<'b>() -> Self::Y<'b> { &0 }
    fn get_y<'b>() -> &'b u8 { &0 }
}
```

Intuitively, I feel like when I implement a trait, I should be able to use the same function signatures that appear in the trait definition. Maybe that's not a realistic expectation though.

Sorry, I should have gone into more detail. When you think about it, it looks like the trait declaration shouldn't even compile
```rust
trait Trait {
    type Y<'a> where Self: 'a;
    // Why does it let us put 'b into Self::Y here?
    fn get_y<'b>() -> Self::Y<'b>;
}
```
The answer is that a `Self: 'b` bound is implicitly "inserted" into the declaration of `get_y`, as can be seen in
```rust
trait Trait {
    type Y<'a> where Self: 'a;
    fn get_y<'b>() -> Self::Y<'b> { // implicit bound
        fn chk_bound<'c, T: 'c + ?Sized>() {}
        chk_bound::<'b, Self>(); // Works, because Self: 'b
        todo!()
    }
}
```
And indeed, the following compiles:
```rust
impl<'c> Trait for &'c u8 {
    type Y<'a> = &'a u8 where Self: 'a;
    fn get_y<'b>() -> Self::Y<'b> where Self: 'b { // explicit bound
        &0
    }
}
```
------

However, what is even more confusing is that this only works when `'b` is not used in the arguments:
```rust
trait Trait {
    type Y<'a> where Self: 'a;
    fn get_y<'b>(_: &'b u8) -> Self::Y<'b>;
}
impl<'c> Trait for &'c u8 {
    type Y<'a> = &'a u8 where Self: 'a;
    fn get_y<'b>(_: &'b u8) -> Self::Y<'b> where Self: 'b {
        &0
    }
}
```
gives a fascinating error:
```
error[E0195]: lifetime parameters do not match the trait definition
 --> src/main.rs:9:14
  |
9 |     fn get_y<'b>(_: &'b u8) -> Self::Y<'b> where Self: 'b {
  |              ^^
  |
  = note: lifetime parameters differ in whether they are early- or late-bound
note: `'b` differs between the trait and impl
 --> src/main.rs:5:14
  |
3 | trait Trait {
  | ----------- in this trait...
4 |     type Y<'a> where Self: 'a;
5 |     fn get_y<'b>(_: &'b u8) -> Self::Y<'b>;
  |              ^^ `'b` is late-bound
6 | }
7 | impl<'c> Trait for &'c u8 {
  | ------------------------- in this impl...
8 |     type Y<'a> = &'a u8 where Self: 'a;
9 |     fn get_y<'b>(_: &'b u8) -> Self::Y<'b> where Self: 'b {
  |              ^^ `'b` is early-bound                    -- this lifetime bound makes `'b` early-bound

For more information about this error, try `rustc --explain E0195`.
```
So the compiler implicitly adds a requirement that `Self: 'b` in the declaration of the method, but it does not make `'b` early-bound. Hence we get a mismatch of signatures whose only difference is something that is pretty much just an [implementation detail of the compiler](https://rustc-dev-guide.rust-lang.org/early-late-parameters.html). 

See this issue: https://github.com/rust-lang/rust/issues/135350

