Simplified example ([playground](https://play.rust-lang.org/?version=nightly&mode=debug&edition=2024&gist=e871d2562b03a8fcc560a68dd20b102b))
```rust
#![feature(allocator_api)]

use std::{
    alloc::{Allocator, System},
    panic::AssertUnwindSafe,
    sync::{
        Arc,
        atomic::{AtomicBool, Ordering},
    },
};

pub fn main() {
    let allocator = PanickingCloneAlloc::default();
    let mut arc = Arc::new_in(vec![vec![1]], allocator.clone());

    let _weak = Arc::downgrade(&arc); // create a weak to hit the right code path in make_mut

    std::panic::catch_unwind(AssertUnwindSafe(|| {
        allocator.do_panic.store(true, Ordering::Relaxed);
        Arc::make_mut(&mut arc); // sets strong count to 0, then panics while cloning the allocator (reallocates because of _weak)
    }))
    .unwrap_err();

    allocator.do_panic.store(false, Ordering::Relaxed);
    drop(arc.clone()); // strong count 0 -> 1 -> 0 leads to vec being dropped

    dbg!(arc); // SIGSEGV
}

#[derive(Default)]
struct PanickingCloneAlloc {
    do_panic: Arc<AtomicBool>,
}
unsafe impl Allocator for PanickingCloneAlloc {
    fn allocate(
        &self,
        layout: std::alloc::Layout,
    ) -> Result<std::ptr::NonNull<[u8]>, std::alloc::AllocError> {
        System.allocate(layout)
    }

    unsafe fn deallocate(&self, ptr: std::ptr::NonNull<u8>, layout: std::alloc::Layout) {
        unsafe { System.deallocate(ptr, layout) }
    }
}
impl Clone for PanickingCloneAlloc {
    fn clone(&self) -> Self {
        if self.do_panic.load(Ordering::Relaxed) {
            panic!()
        } else {
            Self {
                do_panic: self.do_panic.clone(),
            }
        }
    }
}
```

Ok I have narrowed this down a bit. Inside of the proot, you can run this example program:
```
fn main() {
    for entry in std::fs::read_dir(".").unwrap() {
        let entry = entry.unwrap();
        println!("{:?}", entry.file_type().unwrap());
        println!("{:?}", entry.metadata().unwrap().file_type());
        println!();
    }
}
```
And on some but not all files, `entry.file_type()` reports a symlink but `entry.metadata().unwrap().file_type()` reports a regular file.

I'll move this to the rust-lang/rust repo for now but I suspect this is a bug in proot. It's definitely not a bug in Miri.

Ah, the difference between `DirEntry::file_type` and `Metadata::file_type` is that the former uses the `dt_type` field from `getdents64`. So I wrote a little C program:
```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <dirent.h>

int main() {
    DIR* dirp = opendir(".");

    struct dirent* dp;

    while ((dp = readdir(dirp)) != NULL) {
        printf("%s: %d\n", dp->d_name, dp->d_type);
    }
}
```

And indeed, inside proot _most_ regular files have d_type 8 but _some_ have d_type 10. 

This is a bug in proot. You should file a bug with them.

