In this example, even dyn wrapping is unnecessary to get rustc hanging when compiling its instantiation. 

Only under certain circumstances where there is a "down-grading" (or structural decreasing) of the generic parameter, is it possible to make it really work.  Below is a finite, working example. However, as you can see, direct polymorphic recursion via a single fn is still impossible in Rust (since it lacks GADT-style dead-branch elimination), trait-based ad-hoc polymorphism is strictly necessary to prune the AST and stop the monomorphization divergence.

```rust
trait Peel {
    type Next: Peel;
}

impl Peel for i32 {
    type Next = i32;
}

impl<T: Peel> Peel for (T, T) {
    type Next = T;
}

enum Foo<A: Peel> {
    Done(i32),
    More(Box<dyn Fn() -> Foo<A::Next>>),
}

trait Eval: Peel + Sized {
    fn eval(x: Foo<Self>) -> i32;
}

impl Eval for i32 {
    fn eval(x: Foo<i32>) -> i32 {
        match x {
            Foo::Done(n) => n,
            Foo::More(_) => panic!("stop"),
        }
    }
}

impl<T: Eval> Eval for (T, T) {
    fn eval(x: Foo<(T, T)>) -> i32 {
        match x {
            Foo::Done(n) => n,
            Foo::More(f) => T::eval(f()),
        }
    }
}

fn main() {
    let x: Foo<((i32, i32), (i32, i32))> =
        Foo::More(Box::new(|| Foo::More(Box::new(|| Foo::Done(1)))));

    println!("{}", <((i32, i32), (i32, i32))>::eval(x));
}
```

