@ChrisDenton - reading everything again and looking at the deadlock example, it's still _possible_ to deadlock on Windows with a destructor that uses a `join`, but **only** during an application shutdown.

<details>

<summary>Join-on-Drop on Process Exit Deadlock Example</summary>

```rust
use std::thread::JoinHandle;
use std::time::Duration;

struct JoinOnDrop(Option<JoinHandle<()>>);

impl Drop for JoinOnDrop {
    fn drop(&mut self) {
        println!("Joining thread... ({:?})", std::thread::current());
        self.0.take().unwrap().join().unwrap();
        println!("Thread joined");
    }
}

thread_local! {
    static HANDLE: JoinOnDrop = {
        let thread = std::thread::spawn(|| {   
            println!("Starting...");
            std::thread::sleep(Duration::from_secs(3));
            println!("Done ({:?})", std::thread::current());
        });

        JoinOnDrop(Some(thread))
    };
}


fn main() {
    let thread = std::thread::spawn(|| {
        HANDLE.with(|_| {
            println!("Some other thread ({:?})", std::thread::current());
        })
    });

    // ***Here***, because we do not join beforehand, the drops are called from atexit which is done while in loader-lock, so the the binary will deadlock.
    // thread.join().unwrap();
    std::thread::sleep(Duration::from_secs(1));

    println!("Done ({:?})", std::thread::current());
}
```
</details>

I think there are two options here:
1. Bring back (some of) the `Synchronization in thread-local destructors` limitation in the [docs](https://doc.rust-lang.org/std/thread/struct.LocalKey.html#synchronization-in-thread-local-destructors), noting that upon App shutdown (or DLL unload) the destructors can run under loader-lock.
2. Tweak the implementation to **leak in case of a DLL unload**, but keep the promise that dtors won't run during under loader-lock (leaking in this case is all thread locals from threads that weren't shutdown before the unload, and it should be noted that currently only a single thread will run dtors anyway ).

I think 2 is the better approach, and I can open a PR with this change (https://github.com/rust-lang/rust/compare/main...ohadravid:windows-tls-leak-on-dll-instead-of-maybe-deadlock-on-shutdown?expand=1).

<details>

<summary>Sketch for 2</summary>

1. Change `free_fls_key_at_exit` to 

```rust
// add this to `cleanup()`:
let current_key = KEY.load(Ordering::Acquire);

// The key was freed by the atexit hook, so we shouldn't run the destructors to avoid deadlocks.
if current_key == FLS_OUT_OF_INDEXES {
    return;
}    
```

2. Avoid registering the `atexit` hook when `cleanup` is not unloadable (aka, the Rust code is part of the main app) by checking `GetModuleHandleExW(FLAG_FROM_ADDRESS, cleanup) == GetModuleHandleW(ptr::null())`

```rust
fn cleanup_is_unloadable() -> bool {
    // If `cleanup` lives in the main executable, its code cannot be unmapped
    // before process exit, so no unload hook is needed.
    //
    // If it lives in a DLL, the DLL may be unloaded while the process keeps
    // running, so the FLS callback must be unregistered before that image is
    // unmapped.
    //
    // On failure, return true: preserve unloadability.

    let mut cleanup_module: c::HMODULE = ptr::null_mut();

    let ok = unsafe {
        GetModuleHandleExW(
            GET_MODULE_HANDLE_EX_FLAG_FROM_ADDRESS
                | GET_MODULE_HANDLE_EX_FLAG_UNCHANGED_REFCOUNT,
            cleanup as *const () as c::PCWSTR,
            &mut cleanup_module,
        )
    };

    if ok == 0 || cleanup_module.is_null() {
        return true;
    }

    let main_exe_module = unsafe { GetModuleHandleW(ptr::null()) };

    if main_exe_module.is_null() {
        return true;
    }

    cleanup_module != main_exe_module
}
```

</details>

