I tried to build a minimal cross-crate repro from the described pattern on `rustc 1.98.0-nightly (f428d123a 2026-06-19)` (aarch64-apple-darwin, same host as your report), but the plain `[(); N + 1]: Copy` bound across a crate boundary **compiles fine**:

```rust
// crate `dep`
#![feature(generic_const_exprs)]
#![allow(incomplete_features)]
pub struct Arr<const N: usize> where [(); N + 1]: Copy { pub data: [u8; N + 1] }
impl<const N: usize> Arr<N> where [(); N + 1]: Copy {
    pub fn new() -> Self { Arr { data: [0; N + 1] } }
    pub fn mapped<F: Fn(u8) -> u8>(&self, f: F) -> [u8; N + 1] { self.data.map(f) }
}

// crate `app` (depends on dep)
fn main() {
    let a: dep::Arr<3> = dep::Arr::new();
    let _ = a.mapped(|x| x + 1);   // closure, instantiated at N = 3
}
```
→ builds clean, no `<closure_kind> out of range` panic.

So the bare bound isn't sufficient. The panic — `type parameter <closure_kind>/#1 out of range when instantiating, args=[3_usize]` — is interesting: the instantiation args are **just the const `[3_usize]`** with no closure type, yet a `<closure_kind>` parameter is being accessed. That suggests a **closure captured/evaluated inside a const-generic or const-item position** (consistent with the `const trait` + `generic_const_items` setup in #145069), rather than an ordinary closure argument like above.

