if the compiler cannot deduce a generic parameters solely from the trait and the type, then said generic parameter is said to be unconstrained, and this is not allowed.

for example here, on a 16 bit system,it would not be possible to deduce what `SIZE` was used to define the implementation `impl Trait for [u8;0]`
indeed, both `0` and `u16::MAX as u32 + 1` would be valid candidates.

moreover, the compiler is not allowed to look at what you have written for the definition of `U32_TO_USIZE`.
you could just as well have written
```rs
type const U32_TO_USIZE<const N: u32>: usize = const { 0usize };
```
and that would not affect what is considered constrained or not.
in this case it is even clearer that it is impossible to deduce what `SIZE` was used for `impl Trait for [u8;0]`
the correct solution would be to have a generic `usize` and use some form of `USIZE_TO_U32` instead.

i will grant you that the fact `E0207` does not cover the associated type case in its doc is pretty bad, as this is a common case of confusion.

here is how you could make it work
```rs
// NOTE : in the unlikely case that usize::MAX < u32::MAX,  u32::MAX as usize will always give usize::MAX, which is what we want
type const FITS_IN_U32<const N: usize>: usize = const { (u32::MAX as usize)-N  };
trait Trait {
    const SIZE32 : u32;
}

impl<const SIZE: usize, T> Trait for [T; SIZE] where [(); FITS_IN_U32::<SIZE>]: {
    const SIZE32 : u32 = SIZE as u32; // this will never oveflow thanks to the FITS_IN_U32 check
}
```

