I have a set of (related and recursive) types that are transformed through a series of transformations. I implemented this using (a Rust version of) “Trees that Grow” (https://www.cs.tufts.edu/comp/150FP/archive/simon-peyton-jones/trees-that-grow.pdf), where the type hierarchy is parametrised with a type parameter bounded by a trait with some associated types that represent the parts that are changed by the transformations. Some of the transformations require some traits such as `PartialEq` or `Hash`, while others don’t. For simplicity (and because writing the `impl`s manually is error-prone) I want to `derive` these traits, but that requires the derived traits as bounds on the associated types. I’d like to get an error (ideally at compile time) if I accidentally use an instance that is required by the bounds but actually shouldn’t exist.

Concretely, I have a type hierarchy representing types in a compiler, in this simplified example only `int` and arrays with a fixed length `[T; N]`, where `N` can be any expression:

```rust
trait Step {
    // this requirement for `PartialEq` is necessary so that `Array` can derive `PartialEq`
    // however, some types that are used as `LengthExpr` can’t actually implement `PartialEq`
    type LengthExpr: Debug + PartialEq;
}

#[derive(Debug, PartialEq)]
enum Type<T: Step> {
    Int,
    Array(Array<T>),
}

#[derive(Debug, PartialEq)]
struct Array<T: Step> {
    element_ty: Box<Type<T>>,
    length: T::LengthExpr,
}

#[derive(Debug)]
struct AfterNameResolution;

impl Step for AfterNameResolution {
    type LengthExpr = Expression
}

#[derive(Debug, PartialEq)]
struct Typechecked;

impl Step for Typechecked {
    type LengthExpr = u64;
}

fn typecheck_type(ty: &Type<AfterNameResolution>) -> Type<Typechecked> {
    todo!()
}
```

 After name resolution, the array length is an `Expression`, and comparing two types for equality (e. g. `[int; 4 + 6]` and `[int; 2 * 5]` does not make sense, so I used the const panic to generate a post-mono error if that particular forbidden `PartialEq` instance for `Expression` is ever used.

Then, during type checking, the `Expression` is evaluated to a `u64`, after which types *should* be comparable.

<details>

<summary>Complete simple-ish example</summary>

```rust
use std::fmt::Debug;

trait Step {
    // this requirement for `PartialEq` is necessary so that `Array` can derive `PartialEq`
    type LengthExpr: Debug + PartialEq;
}

#[derive(Debug, PartialEq)]
enum Type<T: Step> {
    Int,
    Array(Array<T>),
}

#[derive(Debug, PartialEq)]
struct Array<T: Step> {
    element_ty: Box<Type<T>>,
    length: T::LengthExpr,
}

#[derive(Debug)]
enum Expression {
    Literal(u64),
    Add(Box<Expression>, Box<Expression>),
    Mul(Box<Expression>, Box<Expression>),
}

fn eval_expr(expr: &Expression) -> u64 {
    match expr {
        Expression::Literal(lit) => *lit,
        Expression::Add(lhs, rhs) => eval_expr(lhs) + eval_expr(rhs),
        Expression::Mul(lhs, rhs) => eval_expr(lhs) * eval_expr(rhs),
    }
}

#[derive(Debug)]
struct NoEq<T>(T);

impl<T> PartialEq for NoEq<T> {
    fn eq(&self, _other: &Self) -> bool {
        const { panic!() }
    }
}

#[derive(Debug)]
struct AfterNameResolution;

impl Step for AfterNameResolution {
    type LengthExpr = NoEq<Expression>;
}

#[derive(Debug, PartialEq)]
struct Typechecked;

impl Step for Typechecked {
    type LengthExpr = u64;
}

fn typecheck_type(ty: &Type<AfterNameResolution>) -> Type<Typechecked> {
    match ty {
        Type::Int => Type::Int,
        Type::Array(Array { element_ty, length }) => Type::Array(Array {
            element_ty: Box::new(typecheck_type(element_ty)),
            length: eval_expr(&length.0),
        }),
    }
}

pub fn main() {
    use Expression::*;
    let l1 = Add(Box::new(Literal(4)), Box::new(Literal(6)));
    let t1 = Type::Array(Array {
        element_ty: Box::new(Type::Int),
        length: NoEq(l1),
    });

    let l2 = Mul(Box::new(Literal(2)), Box::new(Literal(5)));
    let t2 = Type::Array(Array {
        element_ty: Box::new(Type::Int),
        length: NoEq(l2),
    });

    // this line should not compile
    // assert_eq!(t1, t2);

    let t1 = typecheck_type(&t1);
    let t2 = typecheck_type(&t2);
    assert_eq!(t1, t2);
}
```
</details>

