@rfcbot concern impl-clone-dyn-allocator-resolution

ref https://github.com/rust-lang/rust/issues/156906#issuecomment-4553666147

ref https://github.com/rust-lang/rust/issues/156920

> Based on the discussion in the All Hands, it seemed like there weren't any features that we _couldn't_ do via the extension trait solution, allowing the base trait to be `dyn`-compatible. So, in this issue, I'm proposing that we remove the limitation on `Allocator` and allow `dyn Allocator`.

One limitation of this approach is that standard library collections which are stabilized with an `Allocator` bound could then not later take advantage of any associated consts/types added via extension traits when they arrive. I really like the idea [proposed](https://github.com/rust-lang/rust/pull/156882#issuecomment-4531150815) by @jmillikin to add a `DynAllocator` supertrait that has all the things that are dyn-compatible:

```rust
trait DynAllocator{
    // everything in the current dyn-compatible allocator
}

trait Allocator:DynAllocator{
   #[unstable(whatever)]
    fn dyn_incompatible_dont_use_me<T>() {}
}

impl Allocator for dyn DynAllocator{}
```

This allows us to have `&dyn DynAllocator` but also allows us to add defaulted associated constants (and maybe types) in the future.

If we can do any kind of specialisation with defaulted associated items, it becomes pretty straightforward:
```rust
trait Allocator {
    // dyn-compatible
}

trait AllocatorExt: Allocator {
    const MIN_ALIGN: usize = 1;
}

impl<A: Allocator> AllocatorExt for A {}
```
but this relies on users downstream being able to override the blanket impl. Alternatively, if we could opt dyn traits into associated items:
```rust
trait Allocator {
    const MIN_ALIGN: usize = 1; // promise somehow that this is "always correct, at worst pessimistic" for dyn vtable generation
    // same as before
}
```

> My issue with the trait split is that it's a lot of duplicated design (where to get some of the benefits of dyn-incompatibility, e.g. custom `AllocError`, we need entirely different methods on `DynAllocator` vs `Allocator`);

I think you'll need that anyway, because the methods of interest in `dyn Allocator` are centered around allocating a block of memory with little control over the details, and the methods in `impl Allocator` are about optimization opportunities unlocked with inlining and special-casing.

`dyn Allocator` is fundamentally `malloc` + `realloc` + `free` plus some auxiliary methods for things like zero-fill; there's no place to put `kmalloc`-style flags or perform useful operations on an error result. The design requires a ZST `AllocError` or else `Result<NonNull<_>, AllocError>` gets too big.

> I may be wrong about the first point, so feel free to type out an API here and I'd be quite relieved to be proven wrong about how ugly it needs to be - but my take is that we either get some notion of conservatively-defaulted associated items so that `Allocator` can be a single dyn-compatible trait with all the associated constant/type bells&whistles, or we commit to it being dyn-incompatible as a trait and eventually resign to our fates & make a `DynAllocator` type.

The discussion around safety of `Box<T, A>`  (spread across like five threads and Zulip...) makes a compelling case that whatever the trait is for `Box` allocators can't be allowed to be `dyn`-compatible, so if we want to support dynamic allocators then there must be at least two traits involved:

```rust
// allocates blocks of memory, you have to deallocate a pointer
// with the same allocator that produced it.
unsafe trait DynAllocator {
    fn allocate(&self, layout: Layout) -> Result<NonNull<?>, ZstAllocError>;
    fn deallocate(&self, ptr: NonNull<?>, layout: Layout);
}

// allocates blocks of memory with rules that work for Box / Arc / Vec;
// cloning (forking?) an allocator allows each clone to allocate/free
// among other clones in the same ... clone pool? allocator family?
//
// potentially grows an associated alloc error type? and flags?
unsafe trait BoxAllocator {
    fn allocate(&self, layout: Layout) -> Result<NonNull<?>, SomeAllocError>;
    fn deallocate(&self, ptr: NonNull<?>, layout: Layout);

    fn no_dyn_allowed<T>();
}
```

I think it's easy to argue that `DynAllocator` should exist, its API is well-understood and well-scoped, and the only real sticking point is whether it gets bundled into the same trait as the `Box<A>` allocator (or if not what its name should be).

Then with the two traits having that shape, it's easy to say that `DynAllocator` can have the memory allocation logic (which is the same in both), and `BoxAllocator` becomes both a marker trait for the cloned-identity stuff plus a place to put `dyn`-incompatible functionality.

---

By the way the wrapper-type stuff is easier if you go the other direction. An `Arc<dyn DynAllocator>` can trivially become a `BoxAllocator`, but the other way around is more of a hassle (and involves more boilerplate by users).

Also as a somewhat-but-not-entirely troll suggestion, you could have `unsafe trait Alloc` be the `dyn`-compatible low-level trait and `Allocator` be the full-featured `Box<A>` trait. I suspect most users who want `dyn` allocation are doing low-level stuff and aren't as picky about the name.

The `alloc::alloc` module even provides a template for the MVP methods (with adjustments for contemporary API style).

