Yeah, I think this is ultimately correct. From an API design POV I don't *like* that you're correct on this ^^; but I don't see a nice way forward here that isn't footgun-prone. The design I propose then would be:
```rust
unsafe trait Allocator {
    // whatever
}

/// SAFETY: Implementations must never incorrectly return `true` on either method.
unsafe trait AllocatorCmp<A: AllocatorCmp = Self>: Allocator {
    /// `false` may mean "maybe but can't prove"
    fn can_free_from(&self, other: &A) -> bool;
    fn can_free_reflexively(&self, other: &A) -> bool {
        self.can_free_from(other) && other.can_free_from(self)
    }
}

/// SAFETY: The new allocator must be able to reflexively free from the current one,
/// and this must stay the case throughout further calls to any of the methods on `Allocator`,
/// calls to methods on `AllocatorCmp`, and calls to methods on `AllocatorNew` on either
/// the previous or new allocator.
unsafe trait AllocatorNew: Allocator {
    fn new_allocator(&self) -> Self;
}
```

I think a sidecar hierarchy can model this correctly.
(edit: This was added to https://github.com/rust-lang/rust/issues/156920#issuecomment-4563160585 above.)

```rust
/// SAFETY: (Same as AllocatorNew above; you can pass pointers between the two)
unsafe trait AllocatorClone: Allocator + Clone {}

/// SAFETY: The Clone impl for this type is a Copy.
unsafe trait AllocatorCopy: AllocatorClone + Copy {}

impl Copy for Container<T, A>
where
    T: Copy,
    A: AllocatorCopy,
{}
```

Note that if we had `Share` I'd call this trait `AllocatorShare: Allocator + Share` and skip the `Clone` rung altogether, since it wouldn't be useful to talk about separately.

