Actually, my previous example didn't allow breaking the drop guarantee for an arbitrary pin type, so one could have argued that only that particular type would be incorrect to rely on the drop guarantee. Fixed version:
```rust
use std::{marker::PhantomPinned, pin::Pin};

use bumpalo::Bump;

#[pin_project::pin_project]
struct Payload<'a, T> {
    #[pin]
    payload: T,
    payload_fac: fn() -> T,
    alloc: &'a Bump,
}

impl<'a, T> Clone for Box<Payload<'a, T>, &'a Bump> {
    fn clone(&self) -> Self {
        Box::new_in(
            Payload {
                payload: (self.payload_fac)(),
                payload_fac: self.payload_fac,
                alloc: self.alloc,
            },
            self.alloc,
        )
    }
}

fn break_drop_guarantee<V>(fac: fn() -> V) {
    let mut short = Bump::new();

    let base: Pin<Box<Payload<V>, &'static Bump>> = Box::pin_in(
        Payload {
            payload_fac: fac,
            payload: fac(),
            alloc: &short, // store short allocator for `clone()`
        },
        &*Box::leak(Box::new(Bump::new())),
    );
    let base: Pin<Box<Payload<V>, &Bump>> = base; // subtype coercion

    let mut bad = base.clone(); // pin box with allocator `&short`
    std::mem::forget(base);

    let proof: Pin<&mut V> = bad.as_mut().project().payload; // destructor must run before memory is reused
    println!("payload: {:p}", proof); // payload: 0x5911354d9940

    std::mem::forget(bad);
    short.reset(); // deallocate `bad` without running the destructor of `V`

    let reuse = short.alloc([0u8; 1024]);
    println!("reuse: {:p}", reuse); // reuse: 0x5911354d9940
}

pub fn main() {
    struct Victim(#[expect(dead_code)] [u8; 1024], PhantomPinned);
    impl Drop for Victim {
        fn drop(&mut self) {
            unreachable!("never run")
        }
    }
    break_drop_guarantee(|| Victim([0; _], PhantomPinned));
}
```

Actually, a great example of how an allocator is basically a lifetime is `Box::leak`:
```rust
    pub fn leak<'a>(b: Self) -> &'a mut T
    where
        A: 'a,
    {
        let (ptr, alloc) = Box::into_raw_with_allocator(b);
        mem::forget(alloc);
        unsafe { &mut *ptr }
    }
```
It converts the allocator to an actual lifetime. If the allocator was just a borrow, then it can still go out of scope and reclaim the memory once the returned reference is gone.

