I reproduced this on stable (1.94.1 and 1.95.0) and confirmed it is already fixed on current nightly (1.97.0, 2026-04-23 and later).

Without `#![expect(dead_code)]`, both stable and nightly emit the two `dead_code` warnings, so the dead code itself is detected fine. With the attribute on stable, those warnings stop being attributed to the expectation, so `unfulfilled_lint_expectations` fires instead.

The trigger isn't the trait or the crate-level placement. The smallest reproduction is a dead type with any `impl` block, including an empty inherent one:

```rust
#![expect(dead_code)]
struct S;
impl S {}
fn main() {}
```

`struct S;` on its own (no impl) already reports the expectation as fulfilled, even on stable. Adding `impl S {}` is what breaks it.

The cause is in the dead-code pass. It runs a mark phase that builds the set of live symbols, then a warn phase that lints anything not in that set. An item carrying `allow`/`expect(dead_code)`, here inherited from the crate-level attribute, seeds the worklist as "comes from allow/expect". On stable, walking from such a seed could mark a referenced item live by mistake. The `impl` references `S` as its self type, so `S` lands in `live_symbols`, the warn phase skips it, no `dead_code` is emitted, and the expectation has nothing to fulfill.

This is the same root cause as #154324 and #152370, both closed by #154377 (merged 2026-04-21). I ran a batch of variants on a nightly built after that merge: crate-level and module-level expect, inherent impl, trait impl, struct fields plus methods, enums, generics, and a nested `pub` module. All of them report the expectation as fulfilled. Cases where nothing is actually dead (the type is used, or it is reachable) still report unfulfilled, so the fix is not over-suppressing.

So this looks like a duplicate of #154324. The fix is on master and nightly but has not reached stable yet, which is why it still shows up on 1.96.

