you seem to be mixing up concepts here, the issue is not  `&[UnsafeCell<T>] -> &[T]`, but  `&[T] -> &[UnsafeCell<T>]`.
this is actually not related to slices, simply to the concept of `&T -> &UnsafeCell<T>`, which SB does not like.
here is an example you can test under miri to see what exactly is happening
```rs
use std::cell::UnsafeCell;

fn good_as_long_as_no_mutation<T>(r : &[UnsafeCell<T>]) -> &[T] {
    unsafe {&*(r as *const [UnsafeCell<T>] as  *const [T])}
}

fn not_good_even_if_no_mutation<T>(r : &[T]) -> &[UnsafeCell<T>] {
    unsafe {&*(r as *const [T] as  *const [UnsafeCell<T>])}
}

fn main() {
    let arr = [const { UnsafeCell::new(0u32) } ;32]; 
    dbg!(good_as_long_as_no_mutation(arr.as_slice()).iter().sum::<u32>()); // not UB under any model

    let arr = [0u32 ;32];
    dbg!(not_good_even_if_no_mutation(arr.as_slice()).iter().map(|c| unsafe {*c.get()}).sum::<u32>()); // UB under stacked borrows
}
```
so your comment that is was not definitely ok is incorrect. both models consider `&[UnsafeCell<T>] -> &[T]` to be totally fine, it is treated the same as ` &UnsafeCell<T> -> &T`, which is what `as_ref_unchecked` does.

the issue is with `&[T] -> &[UnsafeCell<T>] `(or more accurately `&T -> &UnsafeCell<T>`)under SB, is thatthe orignal pointer is `SharedReadOnly` (as it is an immutable reference), but it believes the output should unconditionally be `SharedReadWrite` (as it contains an `UnsafeCell`), which is it does not consider to be a valid retag.

the other way around is completely fine.

