`Tuple` is needed for [rust-lang/rust#29625](https://github.com/rust-lang/rust/issues/29625) `unboxed_closures` and `fn_traits` features; but it should be independent of it. (If `Tuple` gets stabilized before variadic generics, any variadic generics-based `Tuple` definition could just implement the current non-generic `Tuple` anyway; or, the current `Tuple` would become a specialization of the variadic generics-based `Tuple` for zero generic parameters. So, stabilizing `Tuple` should be trivially forward compatible with variadic generics.)

Of course, we don't want `Tuple` to be implementable outside of Rust compiler-generated code. There are various ways to seal a trait; the ones with no side effects/no public leftovers are (suggest more, if any):
- extend a private trait, like:
  ```rust
  /// Internal.
  trait TupleBase {} // or call it TuplesAreInternalOnly, or similar, so that error messages will be clear

  #[allow(private_bounds)]
  pub trait Tuple : TupleBase {}
  ```
- or (more isolated) through a bound to a private trait:
  ```rust
  /// Internal.
  trait TupleBase {}

  #[allow(private_bounds)]
  pub trait Tuple where Self: TupleBase {}
  ```

While `Tuple` doesn't extend any other (public) traits, any tuple types (unlike tuple structs, from memory) automatically implement a few traits if all member types also implement them (as it works now):
